<!-- The palette. One palette, no values-night, and now light rather than dark.

     It was dark, and the reasoning is worth keeping because it was not wrong: a
     television is watched from across a lit room and a full-screen bright panel
     is genuinely tiring. What changed is the brief. The product is being placed
     next to Nepali competitors whose screens are light, and the identity is a
     navy-and-gold mark on white - a near-black navy ground made the mark a guest
     on its own screen rather than the thing the screen was built from.

     So: cream ground, navy structure, gold accent. Cream and not white, because
     a full-white panel at classroom scale is the tiring thing, and 4% of warmth
     takes the glare off without reading as a colour.

     Colours are graded off the logo. Navy #14508C is the book; gold #F5C518 is
     the wordmark.

     THE ONE TRAP, and it is the reason focus looks different now:

       gold on cream measures 1.51:1.

     Gold was the focus ring on the dark palette, where it sat at 5+:1 and was how
     a teacher knew where they were. On cream it is very nearly invisible - not
     subtle, absent. A focus indicator that fails is not a styling problem, it is
     a television nobody can drive.

     Focus therefore inverts instead of ringing: the focused item fills navy and
     its text goes cream, at 7.61:1. That reads further across a classroom than a
     ring ever did, and it costs one flat fill on a Mali-450. Gold keeps the jobs
     it can still do - the brand mark, and the "you are here" marker on surfaces
     that are already navy, where it measures 5.04:1. -->
<resources>

    <!-- ============================================================ brand -->
    <color name="brand_navy">#14508C</color>
    <color name="brand_navy_deep">#0C3462</color>
    <color name="brand_gold">#F5C518</color>

    <!-- ======================================================== surfaces -->
    <!-- Ground. Warm enough to kill the glare, not warm enough to be beige. -->
    <color name="tv_bg">#FAF6EC</color>
    <!-- Cards and panels sit above the ground as plain paper. -->
    <!-- Warm white, not #FFFFFF.

         The ground was already cream on the argument that a full-white panel at
         classroom scale is tiring, and then every card on it was set to pure
         white anyway - which is maximum luminance on a screen filling a wall,
         the one thing the TV colour guidance names outright. Two points off the
         top keeps the cards clearly lighter than the page they sit on (they read
         as raised, which is their whole job) without any of them being the
         brightest thing a five year old looks at for forty minutes. -->
    <color name="tv_panel">#FDFBF5</color>
    <color name="surface">#FDFBF5</color>
    <color name="surface_alt">#F3EFE3</color>

    <!-- ============================================================ text -->
    <!-- 14.64:1 on cream. Near-black, but navy-cast so it belongs to the mark. -->
    <color name="tv_ink">#12233B</color>
    <!-- 5.03:1 on cream. Secondary text is tinted from the ink's hue, never gray. -->
    <color name="tv_muted">#5A6B84</color>
    <!-- Text and glyphs on top of a pale subject tint: 8.9-10:1 across the set. -->
    <color name="on_subject">#0C3462</color>

    <!-- =========================================================== focus -->
    <!-- Focus is a fill, not a ring. See the note above. -->
    <color name="surface_focus">#14508C</color>
    <color name="on_focus">#FAF6EC</color>
    <!-- Selected but not focused - focus has moved into the grid this tile owns.
         Gold did this job on the dark palette and cannot here, so it is a pale
         navy wash with a navy edge: quieter than focus, still unmistakably "this
         is the one you opened". -->
    <color name="surface_selected">#E3EBF4</color>
    <!-- Kept under their old names because every drawable and the nav rail refer
         to them. Gold still means "current", but only where it lands on navy. -->
    <color name="focus_gold">#F5C518</color>
    <color name="focus_gold_dim">#14508C</color>

    <!-- ========================================================== hairlines -->
    <color name="hairline">#E4DECE</color>
    <color name="hairline_strong">#C6BCA4</color>

    <!-- =========================================================== scrims -->
    <!-- A badge now sits on pale paper, so it is a light wash carrying navy text
         rather than a black scrim carrying white. -->
    <color name="badge_scrim">#1F0C3462</color>
    <!-- The same badge once its tile has inverted to navy under focus. -->
    <color name="badge_scrim_inverse">#33FAF6EC</color>
    <!-- Over a real poster frame the picture is still whatever it is, so this one
         stays dark: it is the only place in the app where white text is correct. -->
    <color name="poster_scrim">#66000000</color>

    <!-- ================================================ subject / stage tints
         These are the fallbacks. Real subject colours arrive from the database
         (subjects.color_1) and are re-graded to a pale tint in the client, so a
         catalogue full of saturated hex from the seed still lands on this palette
         without a migration. See SubjectPresenter.tint().

         Used directly by rows that mix subjects and cannot honestly claim one. -->
    <color name="subject_neutral">#E8E2D2</color>
    <color name="subject_neutral_dark">#F3EFE3</color>

    <color name="subject_english">#D4E6ED</color>
    <color name="subject_nepali">#F0DFCC</color>
    <color name="subject_maths">#D5ECD6</color>
    <color name="subject_serofero">#E7D4ED</color>
    <color name="subject_arts">#F0D2DC</color>

    <color name="stage_eced">#F0D2DC</color>
    <color name="stage_basic">#D4E6ED</color>
    <color name="stage_secondary">#D4EDD6</color>
    <color name="stage_higher">#E4D4ED</color>
</resources>
